# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED — the server refuses to start (exit 1) if either of these is absent.
# This is deliberate (TRUST1-S1): a missing secret must be a crash, not a silent
# downgrade. Absence is fatal in EVERY environment, including development.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Postgres connection string.
DATABASE_URL=

# Signs every session cookie — this value IS your users' identity. Anyone who
# knows it can forge a session for any account, including an admin's.
#
# Generate a fresh, high-entropy value and never commit it:
#
#     openssl rand -base64 32
#
# It must be identical across every instance of a multi-instance deployment, or
# a cookie signed by one instance will be rejected by the next. Rotating it logs
# every user out exactly once, which is the expected cost of a rotation.
SESSION_SECRET=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Authentication rate limiting (TRUST1-S5). Unset is the right setting almost
# always: the limits are ENFORCED by default in every environment, including
# development, because a control that is off in development is one nobody sees
# fire until it fires in production.
#
#   log_only  — count and log, but never block. Deploy in this mode for a short
#               observation window before enforcing: the one real risk this
#               control carries is throttling legitimate households that share
#               an egress IP (corporate NAT, mobile CGNAT), and log_only shows
#               you the true distribution of attempts before you act on it.
#   disabled  — off entirely. DEVELOPMENT ONLY. A production deployment IGNORES
#               this value and enforces anyway. An environment variable must not
#               be able to remove an authentication control from a live system —
#               that is the shape of the defect TRUST1-S1 was written to close.
#
# The counters live in Postgres (DATABASE_URL), shared across every instance,
# because Render autoscale runs more than one. There is no separate store to
# provision and nothing else to set.
AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MODE=

# TheMealDB API key. Defaults to the shared test key ("1") when unset — set
# this to the paid supporter key in production (Replit Secrets / Render
# environment) to comply with TheMealDB's production-use terms.
# See: shared/recipe-acquisition.ts (themealdb entry), FS1 G2.
THEMEALDB_API_KEY=

# NOTE — this file is still incomplete. The code also reads OPENAI_API_KEY,
# SMTP_HOST, WHISK_API_KEY, USDA_API_KEY, EDAMAM_APP_ID, EDAMAM_APP_KEY,
# SPOONACULAR_API_KEY and APP_BASE_URL, none of which are documented here yet.
# Completing this file is TRUST1-S11 and is deliberately NOT done by TRUST1-S1.
# The two keys above are documented here because TRUST1-S1 makes their absence
# fatal, and shipping a new crash without documenting its cause is how you turn
# a security fix into an outage.
